# A missing asset must stay missing.
#
# The catch-all below also swallowed /assets/*, so a request for a hashed bundle
# that an edge node did not have yet came back as index.html with status 200 and
# Content-Type text/html. Browsers cached that under the .js URL — a 1391-byte
# HTML page where 1.8MB of JavaScript should be — and every later load of that
# build rendered nothing at all, with no console error and no failed request to
# point at. The build is fingerprinted, so nothing under /assets/ should ever
# fall through to the app shell.
/assets/*  /assets/:splat  404

# Client-side routing: every other unmatched path is handled by the React
# router, so serve index.html rather than a 404. Cloudflare Pages happens to do
# this by default today, but /dashboard and /graph breaking on refresh is not a
# thing to leave to implicit behaviour.
/*  /index.html  200
